I've fixed the false sentence in #1158 and committed it as 24770172. All checks pass: build, tests (7357 passed, 0 failed), clippy with -D warnings, fmt and harness_logic.sh.

What I saw on the wire. A new test, tests/google_max_tokens_request.rs, runs the built binary against a stub server that records each request. It runs in a temp HOME and temp working directory, so this repo's max_tokens = 128000 doesn't apply.
• (a) no `--max-tokens`: the request body has no generationConfig section at all. The guessed 8192 is never sent.
• (b) `--max-tokens 1000`: maxOutputTokens is 1000. This shows the stub really reads the field, so the absence in (a) isn't a parsing mistake.

The issue's premise held up, and it's now something I watched rather than something I read in yoagent's code.

What changed.
• The note: for google, it now names only the guessed 1M context window and says yoyo sends no output cap, so Google uses the model's own default; set max_tokens to cap replies. If the user already set context_window, only the no-cap sentence is printed.
• The unit test checks the full new strings. It also checks that a non-google provider (openrouter) still gets the "capped at" sentence.
• The integration test checks that stderr in row (a) no longer says "capped at".
• `docs/src/configuration/models.md` no longer lists google's 8192 as a cap.
• ARCHITECTURE.md has a new entry recording the old claim as superseded.

Proof the tests catch the bug. I disabled the google branch, ran the tests and restored it in one command, with a NEUTERED marker on the line. Exactly the two google tests failed, by name, and row (b) stayed green. After restoring, no markers remain.

Other providers. I only watched google's requests. From reading yoagent's code, the OpenAI-compatible providers (openai_compat.rs:431) and anthropic (anthropic.rs:641-643) do send the guessed cap when the user sets none, so their "capped at" wording matches the code. I left it unchanged.

One loose end: bedrock wasn't fixed or filed. From reading yoagent's code, bedrock sends its cap only when the user sets one (bedrock.rs:1333), so its "capped at 16000" is probably false in the same way. I haven't checked that on the wire. It's outside #1158, which is about google's wording, and this push closes #1158, so it needs its own issue. I couldn't file one: gh returned 401 Bad credentials at 01:14 UTC on both its API paths, even though it worked at the start of this task. The commit body has a ready gh issue create command for the next phase to run before the push.
